</section></div>
<div class="bottom-bar"><span>Private-sector estimate · Your details aren’t stored.</span><div class="footer-actions"><a class="about-link" href="about.php">About</a><button type="button" id="open-rules">Rules & assumptions ⓘ</button></div></div><dialog id="rules-dialog" aria-label="Rules and assumptions"><button type="button" id="close-rules" class="close-rules">Close ×</button><details class="methodology" open><summary>Understand the rules & calculation assumptions <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="method-body"><h3>Kuwait private-sector Labour Law No. 6 of 2010</h3><p>Article 51: monthly-paid workers accrue 15 days’ remuneration per year for the first five years, then one month per year, capped at 18 months. Daily, weekly, hourly and piece-rate workers accrue 10 days per year for the first five years, then 15 days per year, capped at one year’s remuneration. Partial service years are prorated.</p><p>This estimate uses a 26-working-day month: monthly wage ÷ 26 for the first tier; a full monthly wage for each later year. For daily/hourly workers, enter the equivalent full daily wage (hourly rate × normal paid hours per day); the annual cap is modeled as 312 daily wages. The 26-day convention and daily-worker cap conversion should be confirmed for your work schedule and circumstances.</p><p>Service is measured between the dates entered, with the end date excluded. Complete calendar years count in full; the remainder is divided by the days between service anniversaries. This is a disclosed estimation convention. Adjust service for legally excluded periods; the app does not deduct unpaid absences automatically.</p><p>Article 53 resignation reductions apply to indefinite contracts. Qualifying Article 48 departures may receive full benefits; use full entitlement only after confirming eligibility. Article 41(a) qualifying misconduct can forfeit indemnity. Fixed-term early resignation is shown for review because Article 47 compensation may affect the settlement.</p><p>Articles 55 and 62 address remuneration beyond basic salary. Regular housing/rent, transport, fixed incentives and other established wage allowances can form part of remuneration. The monthly estimate adds the entered components and uses that total for both accrual and the cap. A payment’s label alone does not establish eligibility: confirm the contract, payroll practice and legal character. Actual expense reimbursements should not be treated as wages. Company-provided accommodation and other benefits in kind require an appropriate legal valuation; do not enter an assumed cash equivalent. Variable commissions, performance incentives and irregular bonuses require separate assessment and potentially statutory averaging; do not enter an arbitrary latest payment as a fixed allowance. For daily/hourly workers, enter the legally applicable total daily remuneration including eligible components; monthly allowance fields are not applied. This app does not determine wage components, notice pay, leave encashment, loans, deductions, social-insurance interactions or more favorable contractual benefits.</p><p>Scope: ordinary private-sector employment. Government, domestic workers, oil-sector special rules and other special regimes are outside this calculator. An estimate is not a legal determination; verify current legislation and your circumstances with a qualified Kuwait adviser.</p><p>References: <a href="https://www.manpower.gov.kw/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Kuwait Public Authority for Manpower</a> · Labour Law No. 6 of 2010, Articles 41, 47, 48, 51–55 and 62. The official source could not be retrieved from this environment; current amendments have not been independently verified.</p></div></details>
